From dc3cc809537b2ec54e740b4e1f297ed6df9a2f24 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BlueApple233 <blueapple233_@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 22:47:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] drivers: Add support for OMS match-on-chip sensors

The Jiangxi OMS Microelectronics fingerprint module (33a7:2388) is a
match-on-chip sensor found in several laptops. It is accessed through a
vendor specific USB interface with two bulk endpoints and does not have a
driver in the kernel.

The protocol was reverse engineered from the vendor's Windows driver and
verified with USB captures of that driver as well as with a prototype on
the real hardware. It is entirely plain text: no handshake, no key
exchange and no encryption are used by the vendor driver either.

Transport is two layered: an 8 byte "USBC" request header and a 4 byte
"USBS" acknowledgement around 64 byte "EF01" frames, whose last two bytes
carry a 16 bit checksum. Commands are 0x34 (chip serial number), 0x1d
(template count), 0x1f (index table), 0x32 (identify), 0x31 (enroll),
0x0c (delete/clear) and 0x30 (cancel).

The chip stores up to 16 templates and does the matching itself, so the
templates are opaque and no image processing is needed. The 0x32 result
reports the slot that matched, which allows precise verification and
identification even with several enrolled fingers; it is also used to
reject duplicate enrollments.

The index table is the source of truth for the stored prints.

Tested with fprintd-enroll/fprintd-verify on the hardware and with the
included umockdev test case (recorded with tests/create-driver-test.py,
device identifying data removed).

Co-authored-by: CommandCodeBot <noreply@commandcode.ai>
